𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟭𝟲/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗖𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗡𝗲𝘄 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You updated a Kubernetes Deployment, but the new pods are not being created. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I wouldn't immediately delete the Deployment.

First, I would understand whether the Deployment, ReplicaSet, and scheduler are behaving as expected.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check the Deployment

kubectl get deployment -n <namespace>

Then:

kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace>

Check the desired, current, and available replicas.

2️⃣ Check ReplicaSets

kubectl get rs -n <namespace>

A Deployment creates ReplicaSets to manage pod versions.

I would check whether a new ReplicaSet was created after the update.

3️⃣ Check Deployment rollout

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>

Also check rollout history:

kubectl rollout history deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>

4️⃣ Check pod events

kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

Look for:

🔹 Insufficient resources
🔹 ImagePull errors
🔹 Scheduling failures
🔹 Admission/policy failures
🔹 Configuration issues

5️⃣ Check the Deployment configuration

I would verify:

🔹 Image name/tag
🔹 Replicas
🔹 Resource requests/limits
🔹 Node selectors/affinity
🔹 Tolerations
🔹 ConfigMaps/Secrets

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Scheduler → Events → Root Cause

If the new ReplicaSet exists but has 0 pods, I would investigate its events and scheduling conditions rather than randomly restarting components.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Deployment update doesn't guarantee that new pods will successfully start.

Understand the relationship:

Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod

That relationship makes Kubernetes troubleshooting much easier. 🚀
